-// what they create in queue and fence proxies built the same way, and slots 31/34/35/45/46/50 hand the real objects
-// behind heap, queue and fence proxies to the real device (see the command queue and fence section).
+// arguments only, so the x64 registers and stack words line up exactly. The measured exceptions are IUnknown (0-2),
+// slot 14 (which wraps the heap), 48 (a queue creation, where the PC has OpenExistingHeapFromAddress), 62 and 76, the
+// struct-returning slots 25 and 26 (the title calls them with the C ABI, hidden return pointer first: see
+// "Struct-returning device methods" below), plus the heap proxy's 9/10 conversion from the PC hidden-pointer form to
+// the Xbox RAX form. Slots 8/75 (command queues), 36 (fences) and 32 (a shared fence) wrap what they create in queue
+// and fence proxies built the same way, and slots 31/34/35/45/46/50 hand the real objects behind heap, queue and fence
+// proxies to the real device (see the command queue and fence section).
